  "content": "Long-distance travel from Kathmandu remains suspended, tickets to be refunded\nOnlinekhabar\nSunday, October 5, 2025\n0\nComments\nKathmandu, October 5\nLong-distance vehicles from Kathmandu remain restricted from leaving for their destinations today.\nAlthough the situation inside the Kathmandu Valley has returned to normal, long-distance vehicles are still not allowed to operate because roads outside the valley have yet to fully recover. Transport operators have also been instructed to stop booking tickets for now.\nAccording to SP Naresh Raj Subedi, spokesperson for the Kathmandu Valley Traffic Police Office, no long-distance vehicles have been allowed to leave from any of Kathmandu’s exit points today.\n“Except for vehicles providing essential services, none have been allowed to leave or enter Kathmandu since today morning,” SP Subedi said.\nHe added that those who had already purchased bus tickets have been refunded.\n“Passengers who had bought tickets have received refunds from the ticket counters, and new bookings have been halted,” Subedi said. “We’ve told transport operators to resume ticket sales only after road conditions become normal.”\nDue to heavy\nrain\n, floods, and landslides, the government had decided to restrict vehicular movement in and out of the Kathmandu Valley from October 4 to 6.\nReact to this post\nHot Topics\ngold\nDashain\nEverest\nsocial media\nUAE\nLandslides\ndisaster\nConversation\nNew\nOld\nPopular\nAdd Comment\nLogin to comment\nRemenber me\nForgot password?\nLogin\nNot have account yet?\nSignup Now\nOr use Social Media?\nGoogle\nFacebook\nTwitter\nForgot Password?\nSend Password Reset Link\nBack\nRelated News\nNighttime vehicle movement banned indefinitely on Hetauda–Kathmandu short route\n2 hours ago\nDeath toll from landslides and floods rises to 52 across Nepal\n21 hours ago\nHeavy rain risk eases in Kathmandu Valley, shifts to Koshi Province\n1 day ago\nDomestic flights resume from TIA\n1 day ago\nTravel in and out of Kathmandu Valley halted for 3 days\n2 days ago\nNearly 200 thousand people leave Kathmandu Valley in five days\n2 weeks ago\nMost Read\nVarious ways you can enjoy goat meat this Dashain festival\nHighways blocked nationwide as heavy rainfall disrupts travel\nNepalis celebrate Bijaya Dashami, auspicious Tika time set at 11:53 am\nManaslu climbing in autumn starts on encouraging note; Nima Chhiring sets new record\nMonsoon triggers flood and landslide risks in several districts, DHM issues special alert\nSubscribe to our newsletter\nSubscribe to Onlinekhabar English to get notified of exclusive news stories.\nEditor's Pick\nEditor's Pick\n4 government buildings, including Supreme Court, declared unfit for use\n2 weeks ago\nEditor's Pick\nCharred body found at Koteshwor Bhatbhateni, Gen-Z protest death toll rises to 74\n3 weeks ago\nEditor's Pick\nNo consensus on Hami Nepal’s claim of leading the Gen-Z movement\n3 weeks ago\nEditor's Pick\nDecades-old irrigation projects stall as Madhesh faces drought, food crisis looms\n2 months ago\nEditor's Pick\nNepal’s farming crisis: When plans stay on paper and fields suffer\n2 months ago\nAlso Read\nDisaster\nNighttime vehicle movement banned indefinitely on Hetauda–Kathmandu short route\n2 hours ago\nDisaster\nHundreds stranded on Everest’s eastern side after heavy snowfall\n5 hours ago\nDisaster\nFloods and landslides obstruct roads at 19 locations in Koshi and Bagmati Provinces\n7 hours ago\nDisaster\nDeath toll from landslides and floods rises to 52 across Nepal\n21 hours ago\nDisaster\nHeavy rain risk eases in Kathmandu Valley, shifts to Koshi Province\n1 day ago",
